WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.375
- This video is brought
to you by Magic Spoon.

00:00:03.375 --> 00:00:05.958
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:09.380
What's up, guys? Michael here.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.000
Given the general state of things

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.490
in the live action hellscape of 2021,

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.100
it makes sense that a lot of us would look

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.330
to the lighter, brighter
world of animation

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.180
for some wholesome escapism.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.730
However, it seems like recently,

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.120
our cartoon buddies are going through it

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.880
just as badly as we are.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.140
Whether they're suffering
from addiction issues,

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.330
coping with toxic familial relationships,

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.520
or navigating systemic
sexism, the cartoon characters

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.800
in our favorite recent
adult animation shows seem

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.130
to be dealing with just
about every dark subject

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:38.060
under the sun.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:41.640
So, with the boundless possibilities
which animation offers,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.630
why are we gravitating towards characters

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.760
who are working through real
life traumatic experiences?

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.910
And in doing so, have
we actually found a way

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:52.190
to use adult animation
as a kind of therapy?

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.380
Let's talk about it in
this Wisecrack Edition

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.040
