00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.320
Now, before we can break down

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.170
recent trends in adult animation,

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.280
we need to take a look at its past.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:48.780
Contrary to popular knowledge,

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.060
early animation was pretty
adulty, featuring raunchy,

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.300
boozy, and sometimes
straight-up terrifying content.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.290
Note: we cover this extensively

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.170
in our "Origins of Adult Animation" video,

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.210
so be sure to check it out.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.830
With the rising number of
American households with TVs,

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:06.860
animation began to be specifically
marketed towards kids,

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.190
with the occasional
family-friendly exception

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:10.240
like "The Flintstones."

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.030
Of course, Fred and his prehistoric
pals were rarely dealing

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.370
with anything quite as
heavy as today's characters.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.460
Well, except for that
episode about infertility,

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.330
which was over my childhood head.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:20.860
- We're so anxious to have a baby,

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.950
we take it out on Fred and Wilma.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.160
- [Michael] The show was mostly just

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.890
about a simple fella
who liked to eat, bowl,

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.370
and convert animals into
household appliances.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:30.513
- It's a living.

00:03:30.513 --> 00:03:32.040
- [Michael] A true everyman.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.260
What's more, "The Flintstones"

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.010
was basically just an animated take

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.000
on domestic sitcoms
like "The Honeymooners."

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.600
And even when "The Simpsons" arrived

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.750
to shake things up almost 30 years later,

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.220
this was still the template
they were working from.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:45.309
Not that "The Simpsons" never got dark.

00:03:45.309 --> 00:03:50.309
♪ Like my loafers, former gophers ♪

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.912
♪ It was that or skin my chauffeurs ♪

00:03:52.912 --> 00:03:56.210
♪ But a greyhound fur
tuxedo would be best ♪

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.280
The third episode has Homer on the verge

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.020
of killing himself, after all.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:01.890
- [Homer] By the time you read this,

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.400
I will be in my watery grave.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.830
- [Michael] But the
show was more interested

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.480
in the little flaws and foibles

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.500
of lower-middle-class life in America

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.510
than the depths of human suffering.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.770
And no matter how somber
the subject matter

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.470
of any given episode might be,

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.600
it still functioned in
that classic sitcom mold:

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.930
you were guaranteed that
everything would be wrapped up

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:23.300
neatly before the end credits rolled.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.940
Well, almost always.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.070
- Come on, say something conclusive!

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:29.810
- I'm afraid this is a
very open-ended problem.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.010
- [Michael] The success of "The Simpsons"

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.160
spawned a whole wave of animated shows

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.130
designed to appeal to an older audience,

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:36.460
with the likes of "South Park"

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:38.650
and "Family Guy" making
it explicitly clear

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.270
that they were not for children.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:42.180
And I do mean explicitly.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.210
- Peaches.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:44.468
Peaches, I'm sorry.

00:04:44.468 --> 00:04:45.301
- Damn it!

00:04:45.301 --> 00:04:46.920
You could've at least
given me some warning.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:48.410
- [Michael] But while these
shows were comfortable

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.580
wading into darker waters
than "The Simpsons"

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.810
with whole episodes dedicated to topics

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:55.480
like sexual harassment,
child abuse, and racism,

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.750
they largely did so in the
name of subversion and satire.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.740
This was typically hilarious
and even insightful,

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.670
but which was unlikely to tell you much

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.850
about what it meant to be a real person

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.350
navigating any of the
subjects they discussed.

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:10.490
They were very smart, but didn't do much

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.650
for audiences looking for
sincere emotional pathos.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:14.483
It's the difference

00:05:14.483 --> 00:05:16.680
between watching Kenny
die for the hundredth time

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.490
and watching "BoJack Horseman"
character Sarah Lynn die

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:21.110
once, an experience from which

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.900
I will probably never recover.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:24.950
- Right, Sarah Lynn?

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:27.920
Sarah Lynn?

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.860
- [Michael] Suffice to say,
you're likely not shooting

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.660
to provoke intense emotions if, say,

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:32.980
you're also a show that's happy

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:34.370
to have one of your lead characters

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.280
feed another kid's parents
to him via a bowl of chili.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.960
- Nyeh, nyeh, nyeh, nyeh, nyeh, nyeh!

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.640
I made you eat your parents!

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.680
- The point of this
kind of adult animation

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.610
was to use the looser
boundaries of the medium

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.970
to go completely balls to the
wall, putting things on screen

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.730
that would either be physically impossible

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:53.870
or extremely illegal if done
by an actual human being.

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.390
It was about leaning into
the chaos to mine comedy

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.540
from all the darkest, most (beep)-up stuff

00:05:58.540 --> 00:05:59.830
the writers could come up with.

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:02.040
And it was okay to
laugh at it all because,

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:05.840
ultimately, they were
just cartoons, right?

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.240
But in more recent years,

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.280
we've seen more and more shows ask us

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.990
to emotionally invest
in our animated friends

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:13.660
at a much deeper level.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:16.700
Adult animation is still
as wild as it's ever been.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.600
One glance at "Rick and Morty's"
phone-munching chair-people

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.390
or "Tuca &amp; Bertie's"
turtle-hoarding neighbor

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:23.290
will tell you that.

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:25.600
But they've integrated
some of the harshest parts

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:28.090
of our reality into their
own colorful worlds.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:30.590
The prime example is
"BoJack Horseman," which,

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:33.500
despite its constant punning
and weird animal antics,

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:36.310
is basically a whole
show about depression.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:37.770
It explores mental illness in a way

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.130
that often veers into the
territory of addiction-

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:41.600
- Is this, like, an AA thing?

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:43.270
- No, I don't need AA.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:44.164
- [Michael] childhood trauma-

00:06:44.164 --> 00:06:46.080
(Harper grunts)
(others laughing)

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:47.643
- [Michael] PTSD-
- Oh.

00:06:48.834 --> 00:06:50.940
(ominous music)

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.360
- [Michael] and a host of other places

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.160
where unhappiness can take root.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:56.730
Similarly, "Big Mouth's"
animated middle school hijinks

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:59.280
allow it to dig into the
messy parts of adolescence,

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:00.880
as well as the psychological toll

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:02.750
that divorce can take on a kid.

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:04.280
And Rick and Morty might go

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:05.650
on adventures through a multiverse

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:07.620
full of giant butts and greasy grandmas,

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.600
but the heart of their story
is usually about self-hatred,

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.900
despair, and broken relationships.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.100
And even a show as visually delightful

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:18.210
and formally playful as "Tuca
&amp; Bertie" still finds a way

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:21.350
to deal with painful
subjects like sexual assault.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:22.950
- Tuca, I have to tell you something.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.410
- So whichever present-day
adult animated series

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.840
you're into, it's safe to say
that we've come a long way

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:31.280
from the gentle rhythms
and easy resolutions

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:32.720
of Bedrock and Springfield

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.840
and that where we are now
is a hell of a lot darker.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.930
So the big question is, why?

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:39.320
Why would we look towards places

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.500
full of talking animals and Shame Wizards

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.970
to explore the harsher parts
of the human experience?

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:45.760
Part of the appeal might have to do

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:47.330
with the medium of animation.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.200
Animated characters have an inherent sort

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.580
of universality about them.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.830
In "Animation: Genre and Authorship,"

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.560
scholar and artist Paul Wells

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:56.870
summarizes psychoanalyst Martin Grotjahn,

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:59.620
writing that "the animated
figure works as a symbol

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:03.340
that transcends materiality,
but makes material impact."

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:06.840
Essentially, cartoons
can actually be easier

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:10.190
for us to project ourselves
onto because they're not real.

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:11.920
I mean, have you ever felt more seen

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:13.630
by a flesh-and-blood human being

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:16.200
than you have looking at
the "this is fine" dog?

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:19.120
Similarly, animation expert
Professor Paul Wells has said

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.070
that animation itself creates
"a space for characters,

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:24.620
or phenomena, to operate on more symbolic

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.390
or metaphoric terms and conditions,"

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:27.810
which "invites a greater degree

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.970
of possibly highly charged emotive

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.510
or abstract interpretation."

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:33.900
Animation is by nature a bunch of symbols

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:35.900
that we've created to represent reality,

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.400
so it kinda makes sense
that we'd be inclined

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.740
to engage with them on
a metaphorical level,

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:43.400
even if some of those symbols are things

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.610
like sperm monsters and centaurs.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:48.170
Animation is also far
less beholden to logic

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.490
than most other mainstream entertainment.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:52.070
We'll gladly accept characters

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:53.780
surviving things that should kill them

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:56.630
or entire cities being flattened
and then instantly rebuilt,

00:08:56.630 --> 00:08:59.410
just so long as everyone
involved is made of ink.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:01.990
And on a different level,
it's also less beholden

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:03.940
to the dominant logic of our societies,

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:06.990
so "Rick and Morty" can point
out economic exploitation

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:09.510
in a way that would make
a live action comedy

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.430
feel like a huge bummer.

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:12.263
- Does he like it?

00:09:12.263 --> 00:09:13.760
- It's a metaphor for capitalism, Morty.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:14.680
What do you think?

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:16.810
- What's more, being less tethered

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:18.790
to reality often allows animated shows

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:20.470
to evoke their characters' feelings

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:22.870
and mental states much more directly,

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:24.190
something which "BoJack Horseman"

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:25.850
was always uncomfortably great at.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:27.750
Think about the opening credits,

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:29.220
where we watch him float through scenes

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:31.830
from his life in an
emotionally detached haze.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:33.560
Later, we come to understand

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:35.800
it's his primary defense mechanism.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:37.580
Or consider the incredible,

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:39.840
almost wordless underwater episode,

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:41.920
where BoJack is forced to navigate a world

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:44.300
he doesn't understand
without being able to talk,

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:46.520
emphasizing his emotional isolation.

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:49.440
Or think about the
increasingly abstracted visuals

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.290
used to portray his emotional
breakdown in season 5,

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:54.170
allowing us to experience
the disintegration

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:56.180
of reality right alongside him.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:57.600
In one such episode,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.530
the full immersive capacity
of animation allows

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:04.060
for his costar and girlfriend
Gina's cliche showtune song

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:06.000
to read as dark and menacing,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.130
complete with dancing popsicles.

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:09.290
Part of the reason we were able

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:12.290
to get such a rich understanding
of BoJack as a character

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:14.570
was because the entire
show could be warped

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:16.260
to reflect his interior life

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:17.940
and make it visible to the viewer.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:20.627
- Has anyone ever come
back from this place?

00:10:20.627 --> 00:10:22.120
(Herb sighs)

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.670
- BJ, there is no place.

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:26.810
It's just your brain going through

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:29.030
what it feels like it has to go through.

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:30.380
- And this is true whether he's running

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:32.300
through the washed-out
imagery of his youth

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.570
or watching Diane, the only person

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:36.770
to ever effectively call
him out on his bull(beep),

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:38.900
turn into a terrifying monster.

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:40.190
Of course, it goes without saying

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.670
that adult animation's
introspective possibilities

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:45.620
for achieving subjectivity
also makes it very good

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.610
at depicting drug experiences.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:49.040
Similar tactics have been employed

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.587
by a host of other adult
animated shows, too.

00:10:51.587 --> 00:10:54.900
"Big Mouth" was able to make
a full-blown musical spectacle

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:56.720
out of a young woman's first period.

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:58.890
This reflects how, from
Jessie's point of view,

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:01.100
having everyone know she was on her period

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:03.330
felt about as seismic and humiliating

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:06.330
as being followed by a
giant, singing tampon.

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:07.490
In "Tuca &amp; Bertie,"

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.340
quote-unquote "reality"
is similarly malleable.

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:12.630
When gentrification
threatens their neighborhood,

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:14.760
it's symbolized by a pervasive moss

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:16.770
that creeps into the
birds' apartment building.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:18.730
When Tuca brushes fingers with her crush,

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:21.200
she's so excited that her
bones literally fall out

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.330
and Bertie has to pick them
up, which is what a brush

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:25.500
with a crush truly feels like, isn't it?

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:27.800
And, of course, there's
the show's deft handling

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.200
of Bertie's sexual
assault when she was 12.

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:33.110
The show's normally chaotic
animation style falls away

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:36.500
in favor of a pared-down color
palette and faceless figures.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:38.030
And it's the magic of animation

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:40.030
that lets us truly see the experience

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:41.600
from the victim's perspective.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.527
There's also the various outlandish ways

00:11:43.527 --> 00:11:46.190
"The Boondocks" found to
use Japanese anime style

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:48.210
to explore Black American trauma.

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:51.060
Sometimes that means bringing
Martin Luther King Jr.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:54.070
back to life to comment on
contemporary Black culture,

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:56.320
something that might border on sacrilege

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:57.670
if employed in live action.

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:00.530
And frequently, that means
empowering its central character,

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:03.810
Huey, to engage in
gravity-defying fight scenes that,

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:06.070
in their outlandishness,
paradoxically manage

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:07.960
to accurately reflect the full scope

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:09.750
of his inner power and rage.

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:11.200
When you take a look around,

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:14.140
the anything-goes nature of
animation actually allows it

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:17.110
to aim directly at the
stuff that's the most real.

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:20.340
Okay, so that might explain
why animation is so effective

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.610
at exploring the inner
lives of its characters.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:26.350
But that still leaves the
bigger question: why do we care?

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:28.320
Why do we seem to get so much value

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.110
out of watching animated
characters process their pain?

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:33.060
If you were to ask Ancient Greece's

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:35.390
biggest intellectual show-off, Aristotle,

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.800
he might tell you that
it's all about catharsis,

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.370
that watching characters
undergo tragedy provides us

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:42.650
with a vital release for our own feelings

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:44.440
of sadness, fear, and anger.

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:47.100
And while many parts of
these shows may be symbolic,

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:48.630
they tend to get very literal

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:50.120
when it comes to darker issues.

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:51.360
We might not know what it's like

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.450
to have our fiance stolen by a cyborg

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:55.140
or our wife killed by a version

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:56.680
of ourselves from another dimension,

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.580
but we've got plenty of experience

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:00.950
with the other issues which
affect these characters.

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:04.430
Rick, Archer, and BoJack all
struggle with alcohol issues,

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:06.870
as do about 6% of American adults.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:10.160
And one out of every nine
Americans over the age of 65

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:11.610
suffers from the sort of dementia

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:13.820
that's so brutally portrayed in BoJack.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.970
And over 400,000 more Americans each year

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:19.440
will sadly be able to
relate far too easily

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:22.340
to the experience of sexual
assault in "Tuca &amp; Bertie."

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:25.200
In their journey through the
darker corners of existence,

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:27.170
these shows will almost certainly bump up

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:28.750
on something that either the viewer

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:30.890
or someone in their life has dealt with,

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:33.490
making it all powerfully, painfully real.

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:34.670
But what really makes

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:37.050
these animated shows hit quite so hard?

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.400
It's probably all the
hours we spent staring

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:41.930
square-eyed at cartoons
while we were children.

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:43.700
And it turns out our parents were right

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:45.980
that all that TV time would mess us up.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:46.970
My bad, Dad.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:48.640
Although they probably just thought

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.560
it would ruin our eyes
and attention spans,

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:52.610
not that we would one
day be emotionally ruined

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:53.720
by watching a horse-man

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:55.670
accidentally seducing a teenage deer.

00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:57.900
So actually, never mind, Dad.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:13:58.950
I take it back.

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:01.390
Animated shows carry
an even greater pathos

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:03.200
for those of us who grew up watching them

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:05.190
because they present what Wells describes

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:07.750
as the "fictionalized
notion of consciousness,

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:08.970
which, if imagined real,

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.840
both recalls the playful and
liberal apparatus of childhood

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:14.660
and makes concrete the
irony and contradiction

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:16.010
of the adult sensibility."

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:19.050
What that means is that adult
animation occupies a sort

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:21.550
of liminal space that splits
the difference between

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:24.380
the fond childhood memories
which the medium recalls

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:27.690
and the brutal adult realities
which modern shows portray.

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:30.770
So we come to these shows with
all the emotional openness

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:32.930
of a sweet summer child, only to be met

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:35.970
with the wall-to-wall adult
misery that's on display.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:37.780
And it might even be more than that,

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:40.480
because in our softened,
cartoon-watching state,

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:43.540
we aren't braced for an emotional
gut punch the way we are

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:46.660
checking out a prestige live
action series over on HBO.

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:49.850
So when we watch BoJack
strangling his costar

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:52.060
while in the midst of a
drug-addled nervous breakdown

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:54.530
or come face-to-face
with the crushing sadness

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:56.000
of Woodhouse's death in "Archer,"

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.540
it can catch us completely off guard.

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:00.120
- And he had a good, long life.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.660
- He did, for a heroin addict.

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:05.210
- The anything-goes,
consequence-free antics

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.180
of our "Looney Tunes"
days suddenly gives way

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:09.290
to the absolute reality
of what's happening

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:10.800
to the characters on screen,

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.440
and we're forced to accept
that the wounds being made

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.540
won't just be erased and
replaced for the next frame.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:17.510
They're here to stay,

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:20.480
every bit as permanent as the
marks we make in real life.

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:22.970
Of course, you can always
just pull a Rick Sanchez

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:26.000
and reject this sort of therapeutic
value right out of hand.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:27.950
- I don't think going to a
rented office in a strip mall

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:29.920
to listen to some agent of averageness

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:31.870
explain which words mean which feelings

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:33.330
has ever helped anyone do anything.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:34.350
I think it's helped a lot of people

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:36.020
get comfortable and stop panicking,

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:38.970
which is a state of mind we
value in the animals we eat,

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:40.680
but not something I want for myself.

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:43.580
- But to do so would surely
be missing a large part

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.170
of what these shows have to offer.

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:47.990
We're in the midst of a
golden age of adult animation,

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:51.080
thanks to a bunch of shows
with a lot on their minds.

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:52.680
And it seems like it's the same stuff

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.440
that a lot of us are going through, too.

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:55.870
So maybe we can work

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:58.260
through life's more
painful aspects together

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:00.240
with a few badass action sequences,

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:01.410
bizarre musical numbers,

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:02.840
and outlandish sight gags thrown in

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:04.870
to let us have some fun along the way.

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:06.680
But what do you guys think?

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:09.290
Why do shows like "BoJack
Horseman" seem to (beep) us up

00:16:09.290 --> 00:16:13.180
so much more than shows which
don't include asexual axolotls

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:15.360
and strip-dancing orcas?

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:17.400
Feel free to play armchair psychologist

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:18.760
down in the comments.

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:21.590
Thanks as always to our
Patrons for all your support,

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:24.020
and remember to check out our podcast.

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:25.110
Hit that subscribe button

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:26.760
like you're Bertie punching pastry dough,

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.010
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:30.960
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:31.951
Later.

00:16:31.951 --> 00:16:34.618
(buoyant music)

